  <section class="cp-problem" id="problem"><div class="cp-wrap cp-split"><div><h2>Yesterday, they handled it. <span class="cp-highlight">Today, it feels like all your progress disappeared.</span></h2><p>Same dog. Same route. Same trigger. Completely different reaction.</p><div class="cp-scene-stack"><div class="cp-scene-row"><i>♬</i><div><b>Barking or pacing</b><span>Long after the noise stops</span></div></div><div class="cp-scene-row"><i>♣</i><div><b>Pulling so hard</b><span>You need both hands on the leash</span></div></div><div class="cp-scene-row"><i>⌂</i><div><b>Whining, chewing, or having accidents</b><span>When they are left alone</span></div></div></div><p class="cp-story-pull">You are not imagining it. And it does not automatically mean your training stopped working.</p></div><aside class="cp-stat"><div class="cp-stat-number">NEARLY<br>1 IN 2<small>DOGS</small></div><p>showed fear or anxiety around unfamiliar dogs.</p><p class="cp-stat-detail">47.4% in an owner-reported study of 43,517 U.S. dogs.</p><div class="cp-stat-source">Beaver, Veterinary Research Communications, 2026.</div></aside></div></section>

  <section class="cp-mechanism"><div class="cp-wrap cp-split"><div><h2>Why is your dog fine one day, then <span class="cp-highlight">reacting to everything the next day?</span></h2><p><strong>Your dog does not start every day at zero.</strong></p><p>A restless night. Time alone. A loud truck. A tense walk. Each experience can leave a little stress behind before your dog has fully settled from the last one.</p><p class="cp-mechanism-name">There is a name for this: <strong>trigger stacking.</strong></p><p>Think of it like a bucket slowly filling with water. Each stressful moment adds a little more. As the water rises, the amount of room left in the bucket gets smaller.</p><p>That remaining room is your dog's <strong>Safety Margin.</strong></p><p>Eventually, the doorbell, a passing dog, or an unexpected touch becomes <span class="cp-highlight"><strong>the final drop that makes the bucket overflow.</strong></span></p><p class="cp-mechanism-close">That is why your dog can handle the same trigger one day, then react to it the next. The trigger did not get bigger. <strong>Your dog's Safety Margin got smaller.</strong></p></div>{% if section.settings.bucket_image != blank %}<figure class="cp-bucket cp-bucket-image">{{ section.settings.bucket_image | image_url: width: 1200 | image_tag: alt: 'The same dog beside a bucket as everyday stress fills it from low to overflowing', loading: 'lazy' }}</figure>{% else %}<figure class="cp-bucket"><div class="cp-bucket-stage"><div class="cp-bucket-label"><span>BUCKET LOW</span><span>PLENTY OF ROOM TO COPE</span></div><div class="cp-bank"><div class="cp-water cp-water-1"></div></div></div><div class="cp-bucket-stage"><div class="cp-bucket-label"><span>TRIGGERS STACK</span><span>SAFETY MARGIN SHRINKS</span></div><div class="cp-bank"><div class="cp-water cp-water-2"></div></div></div><div class="cp-bucket-stage"><div class="cp-bucket-label"><span>FINAL DROP</span><span>THE BUCKET OVERFLOWS</span></div><div class="cp-bank"><div class="cp-water cp-water-3"></div></div></div><figcaption class="cp-bucket-note">The same dog can meet the same trigger differently when the stress from earlier moments is still present.</figcaption></figure>{% endif %}</div></section>

  <section class="cp-failed"><div class="cp-wrap cp-split"><div><h2>You did not fail your dog. <strong>The tools you tried were built for the trigger, not the buildup.</strong></h2><p>You might have tried:</p><ul class="cp-attempt-list"><li><span class="cp-attempt-icon">●</span>Calming treats</li><li><span class="cp-attempt-icon">◒</span>A pressure vest</li><li><span class="cp-attempt-icon">⌁</span>Room diffusers</li><li><span class="cp-attempt-icon">▤</span>More training</li><li><span class="cp-attempt-icon">♣</span>Avoiding situations</li></ul><p>Some may have helped a little. Some may not have helped at all.</p><p>A calming treat is often given when stress is already visible. A pressure vest is usually saved for predictable events. A diffuser stays in one room. And even familiar training cues can become harder to follow when your dog is already near their limit.</p><p>Each approach addresses a particular moment, place, or behavior.</p><p class="cp-failed-punch">But <span class="cp-highlight">trigger stacking can happen throughout the entire day.</span></p><p>That is why the results can feel so inconsistent.</p><p class="cp-failed-close"><strong>Trying harder is not the answer.</strong> What your dog needs is support that starts before stress becomes visible and stays with them throughout the day.</p></div><div class="cp-failed-visual">{% if lifestyle_image != blank %}{{ lifestyle_image | image_url: width: 1200 | image_tag: alt: 'Dog resting at home', loading: 'lazy' }}{% else %}<span>TRIED-TOOLS IMAGE<br>UPLOAD IN THE THEME EDITOR</span>{% endif %}</div></div></section>

  <section class="cp-product"><div class="cp-wrap cp-split"><div class="cp-product-stage">{% if product_image != blank %}{{ product_image | image_url: width: 1000 | image_tag: alt: 'Orange adjustable CalmPet Collar', loading: 'lazy' }}{% else %}<div class="cp-media-placeholder">UPLOAD A CALMPET PRODUCT IMAGE</div>{% endif %}<div class="cp-round-badge">UP TO<br>60 DAYS</div></div><div class="cp-product-copy"><h2>If stress can stack throughout the day, calming support should <span class="cp-product-highlight">stay with your dog throughout the day.</span></h2><p>That is why CalmPet comes as a wearable collar.</p><p>While worn, the CalmPet Collar continuously releases a pheromone-based calming signal close to your dog's nose.</p><div class="cp-signal"><small>WE CALL THIS THE</small><strong>CalmPet Continuous Safety Signal</strong></div><p>Unlike a room diffuser, it travels with your dog. And unlike something you use only after stress becomes visible, it does not depend on you predicting when the next trigger will appear.</p><p>The signal stays close through naps, time alone, visitors, car rides, and everyday walks, <strong>without pills, sedatives, or another daily dose to remember.</strong></p><p>Designed to give your dog a calmer starting point and more <strong>Safety Margin</strong> before the next trigger pushes them toward their limit.</p><div class="cp-features"><div class="cp-feature">24/7 support while worn</div><div class="cp-feature">Recommended by Vets</div><div class="cp-feature">100% Natural Ingredients</div><div class="cp-feature">Lasts up to 60 Days</div></div><a class="cp-btn" href="#offer">Try CalmPet for 60 days <span>→</span></a><p class="cp-subnote" style="color:#f1dcca">If it’s not right for your dog, you get every cent back. No return required. No questions asked.</p></div></div></section>

